  },
  {
    "vulnerability": "Integer Overflow in Page Offset Calculation",
    "response-type": "mitigation",
    "reason": "Validate that `offset` and `size` are within reasonable bounds before performing arithmetic. Use `min_t(u32, offset, MAX_LFS_FILESIZE)` and `min_t(u32, size, MAX_LFS_FILESIZE)` to prevent overflow. Replace `offset / PAGE_CACHE_BITS` with safe division using `div_u64` or `div64_u64` for large values, and ensure `PAGE_CACHE_BITS` is a compile-time power of two (e.g., via `BUILD_BUG_ON` check). Add bounds checks: `if (offset > U32_MAX - PAGE_CACHE_BITS) return -EINVAL;`."
  },
  {
    "vulnerability": "Missing Bounds Check on `size` and `offset`",
    "response-type": "mitigation",
    "reason": "Add explicit bounds checks at the start of the function: `if (offset >= HFSPLUS_SB(sb).alloc_file->i_size || size == 0 || offset + size > HFSPLUS_SB(sb).alloc_file->i_size) return -EINVAL;`. Also validate that `size` and `offset` do not exceed filesystem limits (e.g., `MAX_LFS_FILESIZE`). This prevents out-of-bounds access and ensures safe operation within the file's valid range."
  },
  {
    "vulnerability": "Race Condition in Bitmap Allocation",
    "response-type": "mitigation",
    "reason": "Ensure atomicity of page access and bitmap modification by holding the `i_mutex` across all `read_mapping_page`, `kmap`, and `kunmap` operations. Move `kunmap` and `page_cache_release` inside the loop only after full processing of the page. Use `page_cache_get` to hold a reference to the page before `kmap`, and release it only after `kunmap`. Additionally, consider using `page_lock` or `page_wait_for_writeback` to prevent concurrent modifications during critical sections."
  },
  {
    "vulnerability": "Incomplete Error Handling in Page Allocation",
    "response-type": "mitigation",
    "reason": "In error paths (e.g., `IS_ERR(page)`), ensure that any previously mapped pages are properly unmapped and cleaned up. Add `kunmap(page)` before `goto out` in error cases. Use a `goto` label after `kmap` to ensure `kunmap` is called even if an error occurs later. Introduce a cleanup block (`out_unmap`) that calls `kunmap` and `page_cache_release` for any mapped pages. Also, reset `pptr`, `curr`, and `page` to `NULL` after cleanup to prevent use-after-free."
  }
]

